Subject: [PATCH] Lichii #1016: HostTiming waits in the OS (EDEN_UP_L1016_TIMING_SLEEP) and wait stats (EDEN_UP_L1016_STATS); both off by default

Port of zortos293/Lichii #1016 (zortos293, merged as b9bb5a0992; "core_timing: sleep the
HostTiming thread in the OS instead of a hardware wait"), which follows Eglantine #538
(a51de161ed, pretend7979). On top of T85 (0001..0486). Touches src/core/core_timing.cpp only; no
headless derivation reads that file.

Mapped against T85:
- The busy wait #1016 removes: Event::WaitFor's MONITORX/MWAITX and UMONITOR/UMWAIT loop
  (common/thread.cpp:647-702) is inside `#ifdef _WIN32`. The PS5 target (x86_64-sie-ps5) predefines
  __FreeBSD__, __PROSPERO__ and __unix__, not _WIN32, so the PS5 build compiles the `#else`
  branch, a condition-variable wait_for (thread.cpp:704-708). The HostTiming thread already blocks
  in the OS on the PS5; cpu_features' caps.monitorx is read but nothing on this target uses it.
- Upstream's Event::SleepFor is a Windows high-resolution waitable timer and, off Windows,
  `return WaitFor(time)`. Not ported as a new Event method: Event::WaitUntil is already a
  condition-variable wait on every platform, and TIMING_SLEEP uses it.
- HOST_WAIT_MARGIN_NS (0 upstream): not ported. With both halves the same condition variable on
  the PS5 a margin changes nothing; upstream found margins never sleep at 7.6k-16.7k waits/s.
- Upstream's per-second HostTiming stats line and debug-server toggle: the stats are ported as a
  10 s line (below); the debug-server tool, docs and tests are not.
- Event::WaitFor's other caller (audio_core/renderer/system.cpp:448, a 1 s terminate wait) is
  unchanged.

What each switch does (Common::PeSwitch, exactly "1"; read once when the timer thread starts):
EDEN_UP_L1016_TIMING_SLEEP=1: the timer thread waits for the next event with Event::WaitUntil
  (steady clock deadline) instead of Event::WaitFor. On the PS5 both are the same condition
  variable wait, so this is expected to change nothing there; it is the port's behaviour half,
  kept so the stats can show it. Set() (every ScheduleEvent) still ends the wait at once and the
  loop re-runs Advance(), so an event scheduled earlier during the wait is not missed.
EDEN_UP_L1016_STATS=1 (no behaviour change; any arm, the control included) logs every 10 s:
  "Lichii #1016 HostTiming in 10.0 s (EDEN_UP_L1016_TIMING_SLEEP=0|1): W waits, S woken by Set,
  T timed out; timed-out waits late p50/p99/max a/b/c us; in wait X ms/s, thread CPU Y ms/s (-1:
  no clock); CPUs 0x...". Late = how long after its deadline a wait that timed out returned (the
  oversleep; Set wakes are not counted), from a 1 us histogram (p50/p99 top out at 8191; max is
  exact), so the 10 s report costs no sort on the timer thread. In wait = time inside the waits per second; thread CPU =
  CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID per second (a busy-waiting core shows about 1000); CPUs = the thread's
  affinity mask on the PS5. A start line "Lichii #1016: EDEN_UP_L1016_TIMING_SLEEP=.. STATS=.."
  shows the switches as read.
Both unset: the timer loop runs T85's WaitFor call as before (two getenv reads at thread start).

